#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065202} :activity/id #uuid "2c535ceb-c030-5aed-b263-486be04ce20c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773 :topic/id #uuid "bd7555e9-f98c-551c-968c-84222eeed14d" :topic/math-academy-id 294} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065395 :tutorial/content "**Logarithmic differentiation** is a technique to compute derivatives that are difficult to find using the usual rules of differentiation, such as the product, quotient, and chain rules.\n\nLet's consider the following function.\n\n$$\ny = x^x\n$$\n\nWe aim to compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHowever, in this case, we note the following:\n\n- Both the base and the exponent are functions of $x$.\n- This means we cannot apply the power rule since the exponent is not a fixed number.\n- We also cannot apply the rules for differentiating exponentials since the base is not a fixed number.\n\nSo instead, we'll use logarithmic differentiation. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln x^{x} \\\\\n\\ln y &= x\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$ using implicit differentiation and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln x) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 \\cdot \\ln x + x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\ln x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Now, we solve for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y\\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y=x^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^x \\left(\\ln x + 1 \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "21321352-0483-513c-828f-f1c46306015a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6087 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9aa16304-115f-5e48-8777-8b493acf1b51" :step/math-academy-id 37025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064306 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064143 :question/id #uuid "a369d9b2-7637-5339-bd75-5f49b1aac962" :question/math-academy-id "e-9842"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44425e0c-c9f0-5589-a298-071f456fd0c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064649}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating X Raised to the Power of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "5b0b9f01-a558-5a72-80f9-542d588ba830" :step/math-academy-id 37026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064144 :question/id #uuid "bdccebc1-05df-5c7b-a57b-fa50df42a32e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9843"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cc92652-6746-583d-bb0e-53991d5f5e1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064650}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Raised to the Power of X"} :step/id #uuid "2ed78f46-95a3-57ea-b05a-7c9ceb493d71" :step/math-academy-id 37027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064308 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064145 :question/id #uuid "4d047c1a-308b-553b-89e4-9afc5cc9f